{-
   `@caller(source: p)` where `p` is a **dot-call's receiver** - the half `CallerFill.yana` could not
   share.

   # resolver: ownership

   `x.f(y)` is `f(x, y)`, so the receiver is argument 0 of the same mapping every call goes through
   and the expression written to the left of the dot is an expression like any other. The ownership
   stage records where it was written (`ArgSite::leadingSource`, carried into `ArgMap::written`), so
   a source fill naming that position answers the text the author typed.

   The shipping stage answers the empty string here instead: its `ResolvedArg::written` is filled by
   the written argument walk, which a receiver is not part of. That is a gap in the shipping stage
   rather than a difference of intent - `check(xs.isEmpty())` reports nothing about `xs` there - and
   it is why this fixture is ownership-only rather than shared.
-}

fn widthOfSelf(self: Bool, @caller(source: self) text: String) -> Size = length(text)

fn main() -> Int:
    let flag = True

    -- The receiver's own text, which is `flag` - four units.
    if flag.widthOfSelf() != (4 :: Size) then return 1

    -- And the same function called without a dot, where the receiver is an ordinary first argument.
    if widthOfSelf(flag) != (4 :: Size) then return 2

    return 0
